        <div id="aham-vs-saturation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. AHAM Pints vs Saturation Pints: The Number to Compare
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Saturation Pints Are the Headline</h3>

          <p>
            Every commercial dehumidifier listing on Amazon leads with a big number: 180 pints, 225 pints, 270 pints. That figure is almost always the saturation rating, measured at 90°F and 90% relative humidity, which is roughly the air in a flooded building in August. It is a real measurement, but it describes conditions you will rarely see in a warehouse or basement, and it flatters every machine by about a factor of two.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">AHAM Pints Are the Fair Comparison</h3>

          <p>
            The number to compare is the AHAM rating, taken at 80°F and 60% relative humidity. Commercial units still quote it, usually further down the listing, and it is the closest thing this category has to a level playing field. The B-Air VG-2200 is sold as a 225 pint machine and rated at 130 AHAM pints. The Abestorm Hurricane 1250 is sold as 270 and rated at 125. The Abestorm Hurricane 800 is sold as 170 and rated at 80. Halve the headline and you are usually close.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Household DOE Scale</h3>

          <p>
            There is a third scale that causes confusion. Household dehumidifiers have been rated since 2019 under a colder DOE test at 65°F, which is why the biggest portable in a big-box store now says 50 pints. A 50 pint household unit and an 85 AHAM pint commercial unit are not 35 pints apart; the commercial unit is rated at warmer conditions, so the real gap is smaller than it looks. Our <a href="/best-50-pint-dehumidifier/">50 pint dehumidifier guide</a> explains the household rating change in detail.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Practical Rule</h3>

          <p>
            The practical rule for this guide: compare commercial units on AHAM pints, compare household units on the 2019 DOE figure, and do not compare either with a saturation number. If a listing does not give an AHAM figure at all, treat that as a warning sign. Every unit we picked publishes one, or, in the case of the Santa Fe Advance Dry110, a rating at the same 80°F and 60% conditions.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE CONVERSION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Saturation pints are roughly double AHAM pints.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A unit sold as 180 pints is usually about 85 at AHAM, and a 270 pint unit about 125. Compare commercial dehumidifiers on the AHAM figure, which is measured at 80°F and 60% relative humidity.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

