        <div id="amp-draw" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. 120V Amp Draw: Will It Trip Your Breaker?
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Listed Amp Draw</h3>

          <p>
            Every unit in this guide plugs into an ordinary 120 volt outlet, but they do not all draw the same current, and on a job site or in an older building that difference decides whether the breaker holds. Santa Fe lists the Advance Dry110 at 4.1 amps and Dri-Eaz the DrizAir 1400 at 4.7. Listings put the Abestorm Hurricane 800 at around 5.3 by our arithmetic from its 607 watt rating, the Sentinel HD90 at about 5.6, the BlueDri BD-130P at 7.5, the B-Air VG-2200 at 8.5 and the Abestorm Hurricane 1250 at roughly 9 by our arithmetic from 1,035 watts.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The 80 Percent Continuous Load Rule</h3>

          <p>
            A standard household circuit is 15 amps, and the electrical code treats a load that runs for three hours or more as continuous, which should not exceed 80% of the breaker rating: 12 amps on a 15 amp circuit. A dehumidifier running all day in a warehouse is exactly that kind of load. So a 9 amp Hurricane 1250 leaves about 3 amps for anything else on the circuit, which is one work light, not a shop vac and a heater. Two Hurricane 800s on the same circuit, at about 10.5 amps combined, are close to the limit, while two DrizAir 1400s fit with room to spare.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Compressor Start-Up Surge</h3>

          <p>
            Compressor start-up matters too. A dehumidifier draws a brief surge when the compressor kicks on, higher than its running current, and that surge is what usually trips a breaker that is already loaded with tools. On a job site, give each big dehumidifier its own circuit or its own leg of a temporary power panel. If you must use an extension cord, use a short, heavy one (12 gauge for anything over 50 feet) rather than a thin household cord, which drops voltage and makes the compressor work harder.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Running Cost</h3>

          <p>
            Running cost follows the same numbers. A 607 watt Hurricane 800 running 20 hours a day uses about 12 kWh, roughly $2.06 a day at 17 cents per kWh; a 1,035 watt Hurricane 1250 uses about 20.7 kWh, roughly $3.50. That is our arithmetic from listed wattage, not a measured bill. Efficiency differs more than you might expect: the ENERGY STAR Sentinel HD90 lists about 2.69 liters per kWh. Our guide to <a href="/how-much-electricity-does-a-dehumidifier-use/">how much electricity a dehumidifier uses</a> covers the method.
          </p>

